<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panguna mine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1972">1972</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Panguna mine is a large open-pit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and gold mine on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bougainville-island">Bougainville Island</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>, opened in 1972 by Bougainville Copper, a subsidiary of the Rio Tinto group. It was among the world's largest copper mines and a major source of the country's export income. Disputes over environmental damage and the sharing of revenue led to its closure in 1989 and to the Bougainville civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panguna-mine.jpg" alt="Panguna mine" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Owen Winkler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copper_ore_concentrator_undergoing_construction._Mill_building._2_c._1971.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
